Chicken McNugget Theorem

Let \(a\) and \(b\) be two positive coprime integers, i.e.

$$ \gcd(a,b)=1 $$

We want to represent numbers in the form

$$ ax+by $$

where

$$ x,y\geq 0 $$

are non-negative integers.

Some small numbers cannot be represented. These are called holes.

The largest number that cannot be represented is:

$$ \boxed{ab-a-b} $$

This is called the Frobenius number.

Therefore, every integer greater than \(ab-a-b\) can be represented as

$$ \boxed{ax+by} $$
Example: \(a=4,\ b=7\)

Since:

$$ \gcd(4,7)=1 $$

the largest unreachable number is:

$$ 4\times7-4-7 =28-11 =\boxed{17} $$

So 17 cannot be written as

$$ 4x+7y $$

But every number greater than 17 can be represented.

For example:

$$ 18=4(1)+7(2) $$ $$ 19=4(3)+7(1) $$ $$ 20=4(5)+7(0) $$ $$ 21=4(0)+7(3) $$

and so on.

Important point

Don't say:

"After \(ab-a-b\), including \(ab-a-b\), every number is representable."

Because \(ab-a-b\) itself is not representable.

Correct:

$$ \boxed{\text{Every integer }N>ab-a-b\text{ is representable.}} $$
One-line memory
$$ \boxed{\gcd(a,b)=1 \Rightarrow \text{largest unreachable number}=ab-a-b} $$

And after that:

all numbers are reachable
	
